BODY = """
<h3>The problem after R-CHOP</h3>
<p>Before 2017, the only curative treatment for relapsed diffuse large B-cell lymphoma was salvage chemotherapy followed by high-dose therapy and autologous transplant, for patients fit enough and whose disease still responded to chemotherapy. Once rituximab was part of first-line therapy, this approach worked much less well.</p>

<h3>Salvage chemotherapy and transplant</h3>
<p>Adding rituximab to ICE raised the complete response rate before transplant to 53%, against 27% in historical controls [[R-ICE (Kewalramani)]]. CORAL then compared R-ICE with R-DHAP and found no difference; what mattered was the patient's history. Three-year event-free survival was 21% in those previously given rituximab against 47% in those who had not, and 20% for relapse within 12 months against 45% for later relapse, and rituximab maintenance after transplant added nothing [[CORAL (Gisselbrecht)]]. R-GDP was as effective as R-DHAP and less toxic [[NCIC-CTG LY.12 (Crump)]], and replacing rituximab with ofatumumab did not help [[ORCHARRD (van Imhoff)]]. For patients not eligible for transplant, R-GemOx gave responses in 61% but a 5-year PFS of 12.8% [[R-GemOx (Mounier)]]. The pooled SCHOLAR-1 analysis set the benchmark for refractory disease: a response rate of 26% to the next therapy and a median survival of 6.3 months [[SCHOLAR-1 (Crump)]].</p>

<h3>CAR-T in third line</h3>
<p>Against that benchmark, three single-arm trials of CD19 CAR-T changed practice. Axicabtagene ciloleucel gave complete responses in 54% of patients with refractory disease, and at 5 years overall survival was 42.6% [[ZUMA-1 (Neelapu)]]. Tisagenlecleucel gave complete responses in 40%, with a 5-year overall survival of 32% [[JULIET (Schuster)]]. Lisocabtagene maraleucel gave complete responses in 53%, with grade 3 or higher cytokine release syndrome in 2% and neurological events in 10% [[TRANSCEND NHL 001 (Abramson)]].</p>

<h3>CAR-T in second line</h3>
<p>Three randomized trials then compared CAR-T with salvage chemotherapy and transplant in the group identified by CORAL: primary refractory disease or relapse within 12 months. In ZUMA-7, axicabtagene ciloleucel improved event-free survival (HR 0.40) and, with longer follow-up, overall survival: 54.6% vs 46.0% at 4 years (HR 0.73) [[ZUMA-7 (Locke)]]. In TRANSFORM, lisocabtagene maraleucel improved event-free survival (HR 0.375 at 3 years); overall survival was not significantly different, with 66% of control patients crossing over [[TRANSFORM (Kamdar)]]. BELINDA, with tisagenlecleucel, was negative: median event-free survival was 3.0 months in both arms, with a median of 52 days from leukapheresis to infusion [[BELINDA (Bishop)]]. Two single-arm trials extended second-line CAR-T to patients not eligible for transplant, with an overall response rate of 80% with lisocabtagene maraleucel [[PILOT (Sehgal)]] and a complete metabolic response rate of 71.0% with axicabtagene ciloleucel [[ALYCANTE (Houot)]].</p>

<h3>Bispecific antibodies</h3>
<p>CD20×CD3 bispecific antibodies redirect the patient's own T cells without cell manufacturing. After two or more lines, fixed-duration glofitamab gave complete responses in 39%, including 35% of patients previously given CAR-T [[Glofitamab (Dickinson)]]; subcutaneous epcoritamab gave complete responses in 38.9%, with a median duration of complete response of 36.1 months at 3 years [[EPCORE NHL-1 (Thieblemont)]]; and odronextamab gave complete responses in 31.5% and is approved in the EU but not in the US [[ELM-2 DLBCL cohort: odronextamab (Kim)]].</p>
<p>Two randomized trials in transplant-ineligible patients after one or more lines used R-GemOx as the control. Glofitamab with gemcitabine-oxaliplatin improved overall survival (HR 0.59), although the FDA did not approve the combination in second line [[STARGLO (Abramson)]]. Mosunetuzumab with polatuzumab vedotin, without chemotherapy, improved median PFS from 3.8 to 11.5 months (HR 0.41) [[SUNMO (Budde)]].</p>

<h3>Antibody-drug conjugates and targeted agents</h3>
<p>Polatuzumab vedotin with bendamustine-rituximab improved complete response, PFS and overall survival in a randomized phase 2 trial of 80 patients [[GO29365: pola-BR (Sehn)]], and a phase 3 trial later confirmed a survival benefit when it was added to R-GemOx (median 19.5 vs 12.5 months), with more fatal adverse events (12% vs 4%) [[POLARGO (Matasar)]]. Single-arm trials led to accelerated approvals of tafasitamab with lenalidomide (ORR 60%) [[L-MIND (Salles)]], loncastuximab tesirine (ORR 48.3%) [[LOTIS-2 (Caimi)]] and selinexor (ORR 28%) [[SADAL (Kalakonda)]]. Brentuximab vedotin added to lenalidomide-rituximab improved overall survival in heavily pretreated patients (13.8 vs 8.5 months) [[ECHELON-3 (Bartlett)]].</p>
<p>Ibrutinib showed that a targeted drug can be selective for a molecular subtype, with responses in 37% of activated B-cell-like and 5% of germinal centre disease [[Ibrutinib in ABC DLBCL (Wilson)]], and the five-drug ViPOR regimen gave complete responses only in non-GCB and double-hit disease [[ViPOR (Melani)]]. Pixantrone, a chemotherapy drug approved conditionally in the EU, is now rarely used [[PIX301 (Pettengell)]].</p>

<h3>Where we are now</h3>
<p>Summarized at a high level; NCCN placement was not checked against the current document.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Primary refractory disease or relapse within 12 months, fit for CAR-T:</b> axicabtagene ciloleucel or lisocabtagene maraleucel in second line [[ZUMA-7 (Locke)]] [[TRANSFORM (Kamdar)]].</li>
<li><b>Relapse after 12 months, transplant-eligible:</b> platinum-based salvage and autologous transplant if the disease responds [[CORAL (Gisselbrecht)]] [[NCIC-CTG LY.12 (Crump)]].</li>
<li><b>Not eligible for transplant:</b> second-line lisocabtagene maraleucel [[PILOT (Sehgal)]], or tafasitamab-lenalidomide, polatuzumab-based therapy or a bispecific combination where available.</li>
<li><b>Third line and beyond:</b> CAR-T if not yet given; otherwise glofitamab or epcoritamab, loncastuximab tesirine, or brentuximab vedotin with lenalidomide-rituximab [[ECHELON-3 (Bartlett)]].</li>
</ul>

<h3>Open questions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>CAR-T or bispecific first:</b> no randomized comparison, and the best order is unknown.</li>
<li><b>Late relapse:</b> whether CAR-T should replace transplant for relapse after 12 months has not been tested in a randomized trial.</li>
<li><b>After polatuzumab in first line:</b> most relapse trials enrolled patients who had not received it.</li>
<li><b>Sharing targets:</b> the effect of CD19- and CD20-directed therapy on later treatments aimed at the same antigens.</li>
<li><b>Access:</b> CAR-T needs specialized centers and time, and STARGLO showed how results from one region may not be accepted in another.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What is coming</h3>
<p>Ongoing phase 3 trials (listed under Trials to watch) compare epcoritamab with chemotherapy (EPCORE DLBCL-1) and epcoritamab-lenalidomide with R-GemOx (EPCORE DLBCL-4), odronextamab with standard care (OLYMPIA-4), loncastuximab tesirine with rituximab against immunochemotherapy (LOTIS-5), selinexor added to R-GDP (XPORT-DLBCL-030) and zilovertamab vedotin with standard care (waveLINE-003). A dual CD19/CD20 CAR-T is being compared directly with CD19 CAR-T (PiNACLE-H2H).</p>
"""
